  "blocks": [
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Module Documentation Index",
      "anchor": "module-documentation-index"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "This page helps you move from task-based documentation in `gDocs` to the capability-specific documentation owned by each Nodics module."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Use `gDocs` when you want to understand a feature from the user or application developer perspective. Use module `README.md` files when you need exact source definitions, extension points, services, routers, schemas, pipelines, configuration, tests, and module-owned behavior."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Use the [Provider And Capability Maturity Matrix](provider-capability-maturity-matrix.md) before selecting a provider or external adapter. It shows whether a capability is production-ready, guarded, sample/reference, scaffolded, or parked for later work."
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "This page is a task-oriented capability map. Use the [Complete Module Catalog](module-catalog.md) when you need to find every package-defined module, including application, environment, server, and node composition modules that are not appropriate in a short capability map."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "How To Use This Index",
      "anchor": "how-to-use-this-index"
    },
    {
      "kind": "ordered-list",
      "items": [
        "Start with the task guide that matches what you want to do.",
        "Use this index to find the owning capability module.",
        "Read the module `README.md` for the exact implementation contract.",
        "Read the module `docs/` pages when the capability has deeper framework"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "guides."
    },
    {
      "kind": "ordered-list",
      "items": [
        "Use `gSetup/llm` and `AGENTS.md` when you are changing code and need"
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "AI/developer implementation contracts."
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "How To Read A Module README",
      "anchor": "how-to-read-a-module-readme"
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "Module documentation is more detailed than public task guides because it tells you exactly how one capability works. When reading a module README, look for answers to these beginner questions:"
    },
    {
      "kind": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Question",
        "Why it matters"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "What capability does this module own?",
          "Prevents code from being added to the wrong module."
        ],
        [
          "Which schemas, routes, services, facades, controllers, pipelines, interceptors, events, data, and tests does it own?",
          "Shows which layer should receive the change."
        ],
        [
          "Which configuration values belong to this module?",
          "Keeps policy and provider choices out of hardcoded services."
        ],
        [
          "How can a project override or extend the behavior?",
          "Protects framework immutability and customer customization."
        ],
        [
          "Which generated artifacts depend on this module?",
          "Shows when to run build, generated tests, OpenAPI, or LLM generation."
        ],
        [
          "Which tests prove the default and override behavior?",
          "Gives developers and AI tools a verification path."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "text": "If a module README does not answer these questions clearly enough, update the module README as part of the change. Do not make a beginner or AI tool infer the extension path from source code alone."
    },
